# Conventional Commits

```text
type(optional-scope): subject

optional body

optional footer
```

- Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `build`, `ci`. A speed or size change is `feat` or `fix` with its measurement in the body; there is no `perf` type.
- Scope: English, a short noun for the area. Examples, not a closed list: `token`, `crypto`, `verify`, `machine-id`, `client`, `server`, `hosted`, `cli`, `rules`, `deps`, `ci`.
- Subject: English, imperative, no trailing period. The type and scope are always English (`.cursor/rules/language.mdc`).
- Breaking change: `type(scope)!: subject` and/or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer.
- A closing keyword (`Closes #N`) never goes in a commit body; the squash message concatenates commit bodies (`.cursor/rules/github-closing.mdc`). Write `Refs #N` or `Part of #N`.
- Every commit takes one of the types above, the ones in the middle of a row too. There is no `wip`.

```text
feat(verify): reject a token whose key ID is not in the trust store
fix(server): bind a lease renewal to the client key that checked it out
chore(rules): record the constant-time compare rule from the retrospective
```
